  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 253 AM EDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nToday-Tonight... Conditions become breezy and gusty today as high \npressure expands across the eastern U.S. Sustained east-northeast \nwinds increase to 15-20 mph with peak gusts 25-30 mph at times.\nThis will result in rounds of onshore moving showers and a few \nstorms, along with poor to hazardous beach and boating conditions. \n\nRounds of onshore moving showers are expected to continue this \nmorning. The highest coverage will be focused across coastal \ncounties with more isolated to scattered activity reaching \nfurther inland areas. A much drier airmass begins to advect \nsouthwestward through the mid and upper levels around sunrise. \nThis should begin to limit rain chances northwest of I-4 by late \nmorning and into the afternoon. However, moist low level onshore \nflow should keep at least an isolated mention along the Volusia \ncoast. A building moisture gradient favoring higher moisture \ntowards Lake Okeechobee and south Florida will continue rounds of \nscattered to numerous showers south of Orlando and the Cape \nthrough the afternoon before waning into the evening and \novernight. Model soundings show poor lapse rates today, and any \ndeveloping storms may struggle to become tall enough to take \nadvantage of increasing DCAPE as drier air advects aloft. That \nbeing said, gusty background flow may still allow even shallow \nconvection to produce locally higher wind gusts of 40-45 mph. \nOtherwise, storms will be capable of occasional to frequent \nlightning strikes and heavy downpours. Isolated to scattered \nshowers remain forecast along much of the coast through the \novernight, with greatest coverage along the southern Treasure \nCoast.\n\nSeasonal temperatures are forecast this afternoon, mostly in the \nupper 80s along the coast and near 90 degrees inland. Overnight lows \nin the 70s remain warmest along the coast, and areas along the \nBarrier Islands may struggle to fall below 80 degrees.\n\nA high risk of life threatening rip currents will exist at all area \nbeaches, along with rough surf. Heed the advice of lifeguards, beach \npatrol flags, and signs. Entering the surf is not advised!  \n\nThursday-Friday... A mid to upper level trough gradually organizes \ninto a closed low over the western Atlantic Thursday before slowly \npassing over Florida on Friday. Surface high pressure across the \neastern U.S. weakens as the next cold front moves offshore the \nnortheast U.S. coast late in the work week. East-northeast winds \nwill remain breezy on Thursday, especially along the coast, before \nslackening into Friday as the pressure gradient loosens. Onshore \nmoving showers should continue. However, drier air looks to cut \ncoverage back a tad on Thursday, and rounds of more widely \nscattered showers and a few storms remain forecast mostly south of\nthe I-4 corridor. Despite some uncertainty in moisture among \nglobal models, support from the passing low aloft looks to favor \nhigher coverage of showers and storms more areawide on Friday. \nLittle change is expected within the temperature forecast as \nafternoon highs remain near normal in the upper 80s and near 90 \ndegrees each day. A high risk of rip currents should persist \nthrough at least Thursday at all local beaches. \n\nSaturday-Tuesday... The mid to upper level low continues to slowly \ntrack westward and into the Gulf this weekend. A broad surface ridge \nwill extend a weak pressure gradient through Florida. Light easterly \nwinds increase locally each afternoon as the sea breeze develops. A \ncontinued surge of increased moisture should favor slightly above \nnormal rain chances each day through the extended forecast period. \nTemperatures continue to hold steady with highs in the upper 80s and \nlow 90s.",
